torrents of water still flood through  the streets of towns and villages in  swat valley in the north of the country  communities cut in half by the waters of  this absolutely massive disaster  thousands upon thousands remain cut off  here  a huge tourist destination dozens of  hotels are amongst the buildings simply  washed away  just crossing the river is precarious  and terribly dangerous  many have  further south people try to salvage what  they can from their destroyed homes  a cupboard has floated downstream  they've managed to save this but most of  everything else has simply disappeared  where they find dry land they sit in  makeshift tents hoping that aid will  reach them soon  after the flood though doctors are  warning that waterborne disease could  quickly take hold of a population now  homeless and exposed  medical teams are doing their best but  it's a huge task  pakistan's prime minister met some of  the survivors and is promising to  rebuild their communities but he  reflects the views of this country that  the world's climate crisis is to blame  for this catastrophe  the people of pakistan are apparently  suffering in the form of their lives  their livelihoods  for a catastrophe that is not of their  making pakistan produces less than one  percent of the carbon  footprint but is one of the  worst affected areas from climate change  some rebuilding work has begun on  highways smashed by the flood waters  so the international aid is being sent  to the country but distribution is  extremely difficult when the  infrastructure has been destroyed  food medicine and temporary housing are  all needed across the country  in punjab some displacement camps are up  and running but this disaster like most  natural disasters doesn't just end  they often get worse  stuart ramsey sky news pakistan
 

